Or, for that matter: Getting all \bar's changed to overstrike. This is
exactly what happens currently if you try to mix DC fonts with AMS-LaTeX.
The cause is obvious and I can (probably) fix it locally but that gives
me exactly the problem of lack of portability. The reason for mentioning
it here is that the problem is so typical: Explicit charcodes. The code
assumes that the cmr layout is used and it isn't.

I wish I could say that it was due to poor programming practices, but
I find it difficult to see what else the poor style writer should have
done. Now, if one made the new LaTeX contain support for good programming
practice (a \assert macro springs to mind) and a Style Writers Guide
was written containing good advice and warnings then it might be possible
to eradicate the worst cases of this kind of trouble.

I'm not buying the do-away-with-proofreading concept, but it should be
possible to avoid some of the pitfalls through the establishment of
a tradition of good programming practice.

